<div class="ephemera-home">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="eh-hero" aria-labelledby="eh-title">
    <div class="eh-burst" aria-hidden="true">POW!<br>PRINT<br>MATTERS</div>
    <div class="eh-kicker">Clark College Libraries · Staff Guide</div>
    <h2 id="eh-title">Comics &amp; Graphic Novels Ephemera</h2>
    <p>
      The little printed things around comics tell big stories.
      This guide helps us collect, organize, preserve, and make
      these materials easier to find and explore.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
  <section class="eh-section eh-intro" aria-labelledby="eh-about">
    <h2 id="eh-about">What's this collection about?</h2>
    <p>
      This collection brings together the small printed materials
      that circulate around comics and graphic novels: stickers,
      postcards, posters, flyers, buttons, minicomics, zines,
      and other related items. Materials may come from individual
      artists and creators, publishers, bookstores, events,
      and organizations.
    </p>
    <p>
      These pieces offer students and visitors a chance to encounter
      the visual and material culture surrounding comics, including
      the artists, independent publishers, bookstores, events,
      and communities that help make the medium.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW IT WORKS -->
  <section class="eh-section" aria-labelledby="eh-how">
    <h2 id="eh-how">How the collection works</h2>
    <div class="eh-grid">
      <article class="eh-card">
        <div class="eh-number" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <h3>Give every item an ID</h3>
        <p>
          Each distinct item receives a unique identifier using
          the Ephemera ID Generator. The number is shared across
          all item types and is never reused.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="eh-card">
        <div class="eh-number" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <h3>Record it in one spreadsheet</h3>
        <p>
          One row per item records its printed text, type, creator
          or organization, searchable keywords, physical location,
          and donor/date received.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="eh-card">
        <div class="eh-number" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <h3>House it for browsing</h3>
        <p>
          Most materials go into numbered three-ring binders with
          pocket sleeves. Oversized pieces are stored in the flat file.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="eh-card">
        <div class="eh-number" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <h3>Keep processing lightweight</h3>
        <p>
          Transcribe what's printed, add a few useful keywords,
          record the location and provenance, and move on.
          Routine intake is not full cataloging.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WORKFLOW -->
  <section class="eh-section" aria-labelledby="eh-workflow">
    <h2 id="eh-workflow">The basic workflow</h2>
    <p>
      Most items should take about two to three minutes to process.
      The goal is a consistent, useful inventory—not exhaustive
      description or research.
    </p>

    <div class="eh-steps">
      <div class="eh-step">
        <span class="eh-tag">First</span>
        <strong>Sort &amp; identify</strong>
        Group donated materials by type. Set aside anything damaged
        or questionable for librarian review.
      </div>
      <div class="eh-step">
        <span class="eh-tag">Next</span>
        <strong>Generate &amp; label</strong>
        Find the next number, generate the ID, and write it lightly
        in pencil on the sleeve or an archival label—not on the item.
      </div>
      <div class="eh-step">
        <span class="eh-tag">Then</span>
        <strong>House &amp; record</strong>
        Place the item in its binder pocket or flat-file location
        and complete its spreadsheet row.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- GUIDING PRINCIPLES -->
  <section class="eh-section" aria-labelledby="eh-principles">
    <h2 id="eh-principles">A few guiding principles</h2>
    <div class="eh-callout">
      <p><strong>Copy, don't interpret.</strong> Record the text as
      it appears. Leave unknown details blank; put guesses in
      square brackets.</p>
      <p><strong>Make it searchable.</strong> Use the standard item
      type names and three to six practical keywords.</p>
      <p><strong>Keep IDs unique.</strong> Never reuse a number,
      even if an item is withdrawn.</p>
      <p><strong>Protect the materials.</strong> Never write directly
      on an item. Preserve donor information and respect restrictions.</p>
      <p><strong>Ask when unsure.</strong> Use Other for an uncertain
      type, and refer unusual, sensitive, or questionable materials
      to the librarian.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

